ond kernel has started. Using CUDA events in this way can build up a graph of dependencies between operations and streams.\nL155: This graph analogy translates directly into the later discussion of cite55†CUDA graphs .\nL156: CUDA events also keep time information which can be used to time kernel launches and memory transfers.\nL157: ### 2.5.3.1. Creating and Destroying CUDA Eventscite64†# L158: \nL159: CUDA Events can be created and destroyed using the `cudaEventCreate()` and `cudaEventDestroy()` functions.\nL160: \nL161:     cudaEvent_t event;\nL162: \nL163:     // Create the event\nL164:     cudaEventCreate(&event);\nL165: \nL166:     // do some work involving the event\nL167: \nL168:     // Once the work is done and the event is no longer needed\nL169:     // we can destroy the event\nL170:     cudaEventDestroy(event);\nL171: The application is responsible for destroying events when they are no longer needed.\nL172: ### 2.5.3.2. Inserting Events into CUDA Streamscite65†# L173: \nL174: CUDA Events can be inserted into a stream using the `cudaEventRecord()` function.\nL175: \nL176:     cudaEvent_t event;\nL177:     cudaStream_t stream;\nL178: \nL179:     // Create the event\nL180:     cudaEventCreate(&event);\nL181: \nL182:     // Insert the event into the stream\nL183:     cudaEventRecord(event, stream);\nL184: ### 2.5.3.3. Timing Operations in CUDA Streamscite66†# L185: CUDA events can be used to time the execution of various stream operations including kernels. When an event reaches the front of a stream it records a timestamp. By surrounding a kernel in a stream with two events we can get an accurate timing of the duration of the kernel execution as is shown in the code snippet below:\nL186: \nL187:     cudaStream_t stream;\nL188:     cudaStreamCreate(&stream);\nL189: \nL190:     cudaEvent_t start;\nL191:     cudaEvent_t stop;\nL192: \nL193:     // create the events\nL194:     cudaEventCreate(&start);\nL195:     cudaEventCreate(&stop);\nL196: \nL197:      // record the start event\nL198:     cudaEventRecord(start, stream);\nL199: \nL200:     // launch the kernel\nL201:     kernel<<<grid, block, 0, stream>>>(...);\nL202: \nL203:     // record the stop event\nL204:     cudaEventRecord(stop, stream);\nL205: \nL206:     // wait for the stream to complete\nL207:     // both events will have been triggered\nL208:     cudaStreamSynchronize(stream);\nL209: \nL210:     // get the timing\nL211:     float elapsedTime;\nL212:     cudaEventElapsedTime(&elapsedTime, start, stop);\nL213:     std::cout << \"Kernel execution time: \" << elapsedTime << \" ms\" << std::endl;\nL214: \nL215:     // clean up\nL216:     cudaEventDestroy(start);\nL217:     cudaEventDestroy(stop);\nL218:     cudaStreamDestroy(stream);\nL219: ### 2.5.3.4. Checking the Status of CUDA Eventscite67†# L220: \nL221: Like in the case of checking the status of streams, we can check the status of events in either a blocking or a non-blocking way.\nL222: The `cudaEventSynchronize()` function will block until the event has completed. In the code snippet below we launch a kernel into a stream, followed by an event and then by a second kernel. We can use the `cudaEventSynchronize()` function to wait for the event after the first kernel to complete and in principle launch a dependent task immediately, potentially before kernel2 finishes.\nL223: \nL224:     cudaEvent_t event;\nL225:     cudaStream_t stream;\nL226: \nL227:     // create the stream\nL228:     cudaStreamCreate(&stream);\nL229:     // create the event\nL230:     cudaEventCreate(&event);\nL231: \nL232:     // launch a kernel into the stream\nL233:     kernel<<<grid, block, 0, stream>>>(...);\nL234: \nL235:     // Record the event\nL236:     cudaEventRecord(event, stream);\nL237: \nL238:     // launch a kernel into the stream\nL239:     kernel2<<<grid, block, 0, stream>>>(...);\nL240: \nL241:     // Wait for the event to complete\nL242:     // Kernel 1 will be  guaranteed to have completed\nL243:     